WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.080
Recent proposals to expand and reform the child&nbsp;
tax credit have gained significant attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.200
in Congress. When considering such reforms it's&nbsp;
important to understand the tax benefits currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.520
available. The federal income tax provides a&nbsp;
number of family tax benefits – including the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:25.840
head-of-household filing status, and tax credits&nbsp;
for children and child care expenses. In 2021&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:31.280
the total value of these benefits is $ 209 billion&nbsp;
and the average benefit per household&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:38.320
with children is almost $4600. This includes&nbsp;
a $2,000 partially refundable child tax credit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:42.560
The distribution of these benefits is highly&nbsp;
progressive, meaning that lower-income households&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:47.760
receive a larger benefit relative to their income&nbsp;
than higher-income households. Current proposals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.560
would increase the child tax credit and make&nbsp;
the credit fully refundable, meaning the credit is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:58.720
available regardless of income. Therefore, these&nbsp;
proposals all reduce poverty rates. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:04.000
by eliminating the phase-in of the credit, these&nbsp;
proposals would also raise marginal tax rates on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.251
work for low-income households with children.&nbsp;

00:01:07.663 --> 00:01:10.094
Should Congress expand the child tax credit?

00:01:10.094 --> 00:01:14.187
Let us know in our poll. Also, let us know&nbsp;
what other topics you'd like our scholars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.187 --> 00:01:20.285
to cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and&nbsp;
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

